## Context

0146 delivered the deterministic design-contract guards (agnostic, offline,
standalone, stateless, small) as `tests/test_contract.sh`. The **fast**
invariant was deliberately left out: unlike the others it cannot be checked
with pure shell + the Go toolchain — it needs *instrumentation of the corpus
loader* to count work, which is a small but real production change and a
design exercise in its own right. 0146's Notes sanctioned this split ("fast a
lonely third... land the deterministic clusters first; fast can lag").

The need is the same one 0146 names: `erg` is *invoked* (pre-commit hook every
commit, CI every push, agents in loops), never resident, so per-invocation
cost is the product. Nothing currently stops a change from making the corpus
scan O(N²) — e.g. the dependent/ref scans in `check`/`rm`/`close`.

## The guards (from 0146, measured work — not wall-clock)

1. **Parse-once (headline, blocking):** instrument the loader (`parseErgBytes`
   / `loadErgs` in `src/go/erg.go`) with a test-visible counter; assert each
   command parses each `.erg` at most once per invocation. (Note: `check`
   already does extra *raw-byte* passes for BOM/CRLF/header-blank warnings —
   those are reads, not parses; the guard must count parses, not ReadFile, or
   it will false-positive. Worth deciding whether those raw passes also fold
   into the single load.)
2. **Linear, not quadratic (blocking):** build synthetic corpora of N and 2N
   and assert the loader's **operation count** ~doubles (not ~quadruples). A
   ratio on counts is immune to slow CI boxes. This is the guard that catches
   the real O(N²) risk in the ref/dependent scans.
3. **Wall-clock backstop (non-flaky, non-blocking):** one generous absolute
   ceiling on a large corpus, ~10× headroom so it trips only on catastrophe.
   Rule: if it ever flakes, **raise it, don't delete it**.

## Constraints (inherit 0146's)

- Deterministic in the blocking path; the only timing-based check is the
  non-blocking backstop. Counter instrumentation must be effectively free in
  the production path (a plain integer increment) and test-visible only.
- No new deps. Add to `tests/test_contract.sh` and/or a `src/go/contract_test.go`
  (Go is the natural home for the parse-once / op-count guards).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Parse-once guard: deterministic, blocking, with a negative control that
      trips when a command is made to parse a file twice.
- [ ] Linear N-vs-2N op-count guard: blocking, ratio-based, with a negative
      control (a deliberately O(N²) path trips it).
- [ ] Wall-clock backstop present, generous, documented "raise don't delete".
- [ ] `make test` / `make validate` green; suite still runs offline, no new deps.

## Notes

- Decide parse-once's scope vs `check`'s raw encoding passes (3 reads/file
  today: loadErgs + encodingWarnings + headerBlankWarnings). Folding them is a
  separate optimisation; the guard should at minimum pin *parses* to once.
